      "text": "To solve the problem \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor + \\cdots + \\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor\\), we need to evaluate the floor of the square roots of each integer from 1 to 16 and sum them up.\n\nWe start by calculating the floor of the square roots for each integer:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor 1 \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 1.414 \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 1.732 \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor 2 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 2.236 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 2.449 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{7} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{7} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 2.646 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 2.828 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor 3 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 3.162 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{11} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{11} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 3.317 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 3.464 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 3.606 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 3.742 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{15} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor \\sqrt{15} \\rfloor \\approx \\lfloor 3.873 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor & = \\lfloor 4 \\rfloor = 4.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSumming these values, we get:\n\\[\n1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 4 = 34.\n\