      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to compute the sum of the floor values of the square roots of integers from 1 to 16. Let's break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, we list the values of \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 16:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 1 \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 1.414 \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 1.732 \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 2 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 2.236 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 2.449 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{7} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 2.646 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 2.828 \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 3 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 3.162 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{11} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 3.317 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 3.464 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 3.606 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 3.742 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{15} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 3.873 \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor &= \\lfloor 4 \\rfloor = 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow, we sum these values:\n\\[\n1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 4 = 1 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 4 + 3 \\cdot 5 + 4 = 5 + 8 + 15 + 4 = 32.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{32}\\).",
